    description: "Three low-severity cleanups from the 2026-07-15 tier-axis concept audit (bundled):\n\nF5 — dead config: SupplyChainConfig.analysis_tiers (supply_chain.py:164, default [1,2,3]) is defined, serialized (to_dict), and parsed (from_dict) but consumed NOWHERE — a phantom knob. The real tier filter is --max-tier / effective_tier (cli.py:9246). A user setting analysis_tiers gets no effect. FIX: remove it, or wire it to the tier filter.\n\nF6 — docstring drift: several docstrings describe tier-2 as \"internal-dep SOURCE code\" (ranking.py:394 \"internal deps (tier 2) get 1.5x\", cfg.py:1686-1687, supply_chain.py:10-14) — but tier-2 is essentially never a plain file-path verdict (_classify_file_core emits only 1/3/4 by default; tier-2 comes only from config internal_package_roots + workspace-sibling dependency declarations + six synthetic linkers). The concept the weight table documents rarely exists. FIX: correct the docstrings to describe what tier-2 actually contains.\n\nF7 — stale comments: jupyter.py:28 says notebooks are placed at \"Tier 2 (INTERNAL_DEP)\" but they are tier-1 since INV-naduh (classify_file returns FIRST_PARTY for .ipynb; no live tier-2 producer in jupyter.py). py_deps.py:313-322 describes the RETIRED pre-ADR-0041 \"external tier-2 direct dependency\" semantics (the code — subtracting workspace import-names — is still correct; the comment's \"external tier-2\" is stale).\n\nEvidence: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/concept-audit-supply_chain_tier_07152026.md (F5/F6/F7)."  # d67a
